The client answers, closes the browser, comes back, and nothing is lost. That
was the phase's definition of done, and the mock could never satisfy it.

The form is rendered from the template: sections, questions, types, options and
required flags all come from Postgres. `src/mocks/hartmann.ts` no longer has an
`ONBOARDING`, `getOnboarding()` is gone from the data layer, and the prototype
component — with its "answers are not saved" label and its local `done` flag —
was deleted. Whether the onboarding was submitted is now read from the database,
so a refresh keeps showing the confirmation.

Autosave has three triggers, because a debounce alone loses answers in two real
situations on a phone: typing then tapping Continue, and typing then leaving for
another app. So: debounce while typing, blur on leaving a field, and a mandatory
flush before changing section or submitting. A save that failed goes back to the
queue and blocks the submit — sending a submission knowing an answer was left
behind is the most expensive way to lose data on this screen.

Emptiness is decided per TYPE, never by truthiness: `false` answers a boolean
and `0` answers a number, and both are falsy in JavaScript. The same rule lives
in SQL, and the two are compared case by case in the tests.

Four states, and they are genuinely different: `unsupported` (this journey has
no onboarding stage), `not_started` (Boop has not opened it), `draft`,
`submitted`. None of them is a 404.

Admin gets an Onboarding section inside the project page — not a new route and
not a new dashboard: open the form when the stage allows it, say what is missing
when it does not, read the answers as Section → Question → Answer, and reopen.

Portal navigation still has seven items.

